    body: "# Seton / EMEDCO Primer — the account group you're contributing to\n\nSeton and EMEDCO are the second account group on your ramp — you're a **contributor** here\n(audit, propose, help), not an owner, so this primer stays at orientation level: what the\nbusiness is, how the two accounts differ, and the things that will trip up a newcomer.\nRead [`ppc-fundamentals.md`](./ppc-fundamentals.md) first if you haven't.\n\n---\n\n## What Seton and EMEDCO are\n\nSeton and EMEDCO sit alongside Brady and PDC as one of the team's account groups, run\nunder a separate manager account (see the MCC-naming trap in\n[`cheat-sheet.md`](./cheat-sheet.md) — the MCC that holds these two is confusingly named\n\"Brady US - MCC (Seton/Emed)\" and does **not** contain the actual Brady US account).\n\nBoth sell industrial safety and identification products — think pipe & valve markers,\nsafety and facility signs, traffic control products, labels, tags, and lockout/tagout\ngear — to a B2B buyer. **Seton** (seton.com) is the larger of the two; **EMEDCO**\n(emedco.com) is smaller and runs a leaner, more Shopping-heavy program. Both are separate\nGoogle Ads accounts, run together under the same manager account alongside Seton Canada.\n\n---\n\n## How the two accounts differ\n\n| | Seton US | EMEDCO |\n|---|---|---|\n| Size | The larger account | Smaller |\n| Channel mix | A real two-engine account — Search and Shopping run at comparable scale | Shopping-first, with a small brand/search layer on top |\n| Timezone | Eastern | Central |\n| What that means for you | More search structure to learn (match-type splits, DSA coverage) | Simpler shape, but don't mistake \"simpler\" for \"smaller effort\" — Shopping tuning still matters a lot here |\n\n**Timezone mismatch matters:** Seton reports on Eastern time, EMEDCO on Central. If you're\ncomparing \"yesterday's\" numbers across the two, or scheduling anything, account for the\ngap — it's a real source of confusing day-over-day comparisons for a newcomer.\n\n---\n\n## ⚠️ Before you trust a number on this account group\n\nThe same rule from [`how-brady-measures.md`](./how-brady-measures.md) applies here, with\nan extra wrinkle: **platform ROAS on Seton/EMEDCO is not a clean revenue signal.** These\ntwo accounts bid on a blended mix that includes actual purchases *and* placeholder values\nfor leads (quote requests, catalog requests, phone calls) *and* an offline-value import.\nThat mix inflates and complicates the platform ROAS number well beyond the usual\nfirst-touch-vs-platform gap. **Use platform ROAS to compare campaigns against each other,\nnever to report as revenue.** For real revenue, go through the same first-touch,\nBigQuery/Adobe path as everywhere else.\n\nSeton and EMEDCO also each carry **a large amount of paused and legacy campaign\nstructure** underneath the live accounts — old builds from earlier campaign-management\ngenerations that are switched off but still present. **Never bulk-enable anything paused\nwithout checking with Alex or the account lead first** — some of it is inert history, but\nsome of it exists for reasons that aren't obvious just from the name.\n\n---\n\n## What \"good\" looks like on this account group\n\n- Seton runs closer to breakeven on platform ROAS than Brady's flagship accounts do —\n  that's expected, not a red flag. Efficiency profiles differ a lot by account group; see\n  [`how-brady-measures.md`](./how-brady-measures.md) for the guardrail concept and don't\n  compare Seton/EMEDCO's numbers raw against Brady's.\n- EMEDCO tends to run more efficiently than Seton on platform ROAS, partly because it\n  leans harder on Shopping and carries a smaller, thinner search layer.\n- Brand campaigns on both accounts are intentionally throttled in places — a low ROAS on\n  a brand campaign here can be a deliberate spend cap, not underperformance. Ask before\n  assuming something's broken.\n\n---\n\n## Where to go deeper\n\nThis primer is deliberately light — Seton/EMEDCO is a big, busy account group, and the\nfull operational picture (campaign naming conventions, budget-tier structure, negative-list\narchitecture, audience setup) is something you'll pick up hands-on as you start\ncontributing, not something to memorize up front. When you're ready to go deeper:\n\n- Ask **Alex** or your account lead for a walkthrough of the live campaign list — the\n  campaigns that actually spend meaningfully are a small fraction of what's in the account.\n- **Ben** (SEO) has worked on both PDC and Seton/EMEDCO and is worth an early conversation\n  about the business side (see\n  [`../01-start-here/how-the-ramp-works.md`](../01-start-here/how-the-ramp-works.md)).\n- Bring specific questions to your 1:1s rather than trying to absorb the whole account\n  structure solo — this group has a lot of history, and the fastest way through it is\n  asking someone who was there."
