    body: "# How to run a project\n\nEvery brief tells you *what to do*. This is the part around it — what to sort out before you\nstart, and what happens once the deliverable exists.\n\n**Read this once. After that it's about five minutes of habit per project.**\n\n| | |\n|---|---|\n| **Before** | Write your prediction. Check the scope. Get your screens ready. Pick the right time |\n| **During** | Work the brief. Treat the timebox as data |\n| **After** | Write it up the same day → readout → file the loose ends → log what you got wrong |\n\n---\n\n## Before\n\n### 1 · Write down what you think the answer is — *before you open anything*\n\nThis is the one that actually matters, and it only works in that order.\n\nMost briefs are built on **predict-then-compare**: you make your call, then you see the\nanswer, then you explain the difference. The learning is entirely in that third step.\n\n**But a prediction written after you've peeked isn't a prediction.** You'll unconsciously\nadjust it, decide you basically knew, and skip the only part that teaches anything. That's\nnot a discipline problem, it's how memory works — everyone does it unless the prediction is\nalready written down.\n\nSo: read the brief, close it, and write two or three lines in your project file first.\n\n> *\"I think PDC Healthcare will be mostly search with one PMax. I'd guess brand is a small\n> share of spend. I have no idea what it optimizes to.\"*\n\nThirty seconds. **\"I have no idea\" is a legitimate and useful prediction** — it makes the\ngap visible later, which is the whole point.\n\n### 2 · Check what you're *not* doing\n\nMost briefs have a boundary in them and it's easy to blow past. T1-1 tells you to notice\nwhat each campaign optimizes to **and move on** — the proper investigation is T1-4. Going\ndeep there feels productive and turns a one-hour project into three.\n\n**If the brief doesn't say where the edge is, ask before you start**, not at hour two.\n\n### 3 · Get your screens ready\n\nSounds trivial; it's twenty minutes every time it isn't done. Before you start the clock:\nthe right account open, the right date range set, the right report found, and your project\nfile already created in `04-my-work/projects/`.\n\n### 4 · Pick the right time of day\n\n| Rule | Why |\n|---|---|\n| **Don't ship a live change late on a Friday** | If it does something unexpected, you want people around. This is a normal professional habit, not a rule about you |\n| **Don't ship one right before you're out** | Same reason. You should be there for the day after |\n| **Do the analysis work when you're fresh** | The keep/kill and inventory projects are attention work. They go badly at 4:30pm |\n\n---\n\n## What a paired session is actually like\n\nNobody tells new people this, so here it is.\n\n**Alex will let you run.** He isn't waiting to catch you out, and being wrong in a paired\nsession costs nothing — that's precisely why the projects where a wrong mental model is\nexpensive to unlearn are the paired ones.\n\nHe'll interrupt if you're heading somewhere that would waste real time or money. Otherwise\nhe'll mostly let you get there yourself, because that's what makes it stick.\n\n**Your job in a paired session is to think out loud.** Silence looks like understanding and\nisn't. Say the half-formed version — \"I think this is the brand one because…\" — even when\nyou're not sure. Especially then.\n\n**Ask at least two questions.** If you got through an hour inside an account you'd never\nseen without a question, you were performing rather than learning.\n\n---\n\n## After\n\n### 1 · Write it up the same day\n\nNot because of a deadline — because **you will lose the reasoning by tomorrow.** You'll keep\nthe conclusion and lose why you believed it, and the why is the part worth having.\n\nIf the day gets away from you, write five bullets before you log off and finish it in the\nmorning. Five bullets same-day beats a polished page two days later.\n\n### 2 · The readout: three parts, ten minutes\n\nSeveral projects end with a short readout. There's a version of this that wastes everyone's\ntime, and it's the version almost everyone does first:\n\n🔴 **The common one:** a narration of what you did. *\"I opened the account, I filtered to 30\ndays, I sorted by cost, then I looked at each campaign…\"* Nobody needs the walkthrough.\n\n🟢 **The useful one — three parts:**\n\n| Part | One line each |\n|---|---|\n| **What I found** | The conclusion, first. Not the method |\n| **What I was wrong about** | Your prediction vs. what was actually there, and what caused the gap |\n| **What I'd do next** | One thing. Even if it's \"I'd want to check X\" |\n\nThat middle part is the one people leave out, and it's the one worth hearing. **A readout\nwith no surprise in it usually means the project was too easy or you didn't look hard\nenough.**\n\n### 3 · File the loose ends — don't leave them in the document\n\nEvery project turns up things you noticed but didn't chase. Right now they die in your\nwrite-up. Give each one a destination:\n\n| What it is | Where it goes |\n|---|---|\n| Something you didn't understand | `04-my-work/questions/` |\n| Something that looks broken | Tell Alex. Don't sit on it because you're not certain — \"this looks wrong, I might be misreading it\" is a complete and welcome sentence |\n| Something worth doing later | Say it in your readout so it can become a real to-do with an owner |\n| Something worth testing | Same — test-shaped ideas have their own home, and Alex will route it |\n\n### 4 · Log what you got wrong\n\n`04-my-work/what-i-got-wrong/` — one running file, one line per surprise, all 90 days.\n\n**This is the highest-value thing you'll produce during the ramp**, and it takes about a\nminute per project. Two reasons:\n\n- **Patterns show up that no single project reveals.** Four entries in, you'll notice you\n  consistently assume the platform number is the real number, or that you keep expecting\n  brand and non-brand to behave the same way. That's a fixable habit — but only once you can\n  see it.\n- **It's the evidence for your Day-90 re-score.** When you fill in\n  [`baseline.md`](../05-self-assessment/baseline.md) again, this file is what stops the\n  exercise being a vague feeling about whether you improved.\n\n**Nobody grades it and nobody else has to read it.** Write it for yourself and be blunt.\n\n### 5 · Say whether the timebox held\n\nIf a one-hour project took three, **say so.** That's a scoping error on our side and it's\nuseful information — the ladder gets fixed with that feedback and stays wrong without it.\n\nThe same goes the other way: if a half-day project took forty minutes, that's worth knowing\ntoo.\n\n---\n\n## What happens to your deliverable\n\n**Alex reads it. It isn't graded, and it doesn't go into any review.** It sits in your folder\nas a record of your own thinking, and the genuinely useful ones get reused — a good structure\nmap is the thing the next person reads instead of starting cold.\n\nThat's the actual reason every project has a deliverable. Not to prove you did the work — to\nleave something behind."
  },
  {
    id: "02-learning/what-a-deliverable-looks-like",
    title: "What a deliverable looks like",
    folder: "Learning",
    kind: "Learning",
    projectId: "",
    source: "02-learning/what-a-deliverable-looks-like.md",
    summary: "Nearly every project asks you to produce something — \"a one-page structure map,\" \"an",
    order: 160,
    words: 1015,
    body: "# What a deliverable looks like\n\nNearly every project asks you to produce something — \"a one-page structure map,\" \"an\nannotated 30-row list,\" \"five lines.\" **You've never seen one of those**, which makes the\nfirst few a guessing game. This file removes the guessing.\n\n> ## ⚠️ The account below is invented\n>\n> **\"Northwind Safety Supply\" is not a Brady account and none of these numbers are real.**\n> They were made up for this example, deliberately, so that nothing in here can ever be\n> mistaken for a real figure or quoted by accident.\n>\n> This is the one place in the repo where you'll see fabricated numbers, and it's labeled\n> this loudly on purpose. **Everywhere else, a number you can't source is a number you\n> don't use.**\n\n---\n\n## Worked example — a structure map\n\nThis is the T1-1 deliverable: open an account, list what's actually spending, and describe\nwhat it's doing.\n\n### Northwind Safety Supply — account structure, last 30 days\n\n*Pulled 12 Mar, last 30 days, sorted by cost. Paused campaigns excluded.*\n\n| Campaign | Type | Bidding | Optimizing to | Brand / NB |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| NW-GGL-US-[B]-Core | Search | tROAS, target 600% | Online purchase (primary) | Brand |\n| NW-GGL-US-[NB]-Signage | Search | tROAS, target 350% | Online purchase (primary) | Non-brand |\n| NW-GGL-US-[NB]-LOTO | Search | Max conversions, no target | Online purchase + **Quote request** | Non-brand |\n| NW-GGL-US-PMax-Retail | PMax (retail, feed) | tROAS, target 400% | Online purchase (primary) | Mixed |\n| NW-GGL-US-[NB]-DSA | DSA | Max clicks | Online purchase (primary) | Non-brand |\n\n**How I told brand from non-brand:** the `[B]`/`[NB]` tag in the campaign name, checked\nagainst the keywords — the brand campaign is all variations of the company name, and the\nnon-brand ones don't contain it anywhere. The PMax campaign is marked mixed because it has\nno keywords to check and its search themes cover both.\n\n**What this account is trying to do:** drive online purchases of safety signage and\nlockout/tagout products, mostly through non-brand search, with a brand campaign defending\nthe name cheaply.\n\n**How I'd know if it stopped:** purchase volume on the two non-brand search campaigns is the\nnumber to watch. Brand would keep converting for a while even if non-brand collapsed, so\ntotal account conversions would hide the problem for a few weeks.\n\n### ⚠️ Three things I noticed but didn't chase\n\n1. **The LOTO campaign is optimizing to two actions**, one of which is a quote request. A\n   quote and a purchase aren't worth the same, so I'd want to know whether that's deliberate.\n2. **The DSA campaign is on Max clicks** while everything else is on a value target. That\n   might be intentional for coverage, but it means it's chasing traffic rather than revenue.\n3. **PMax has no target on its brand exclusions** that I could find, so it may be absorbing\n   brand traffic the brand campaign is also paying for.\n\n---\n\n## Why that's a good deliverable\n\n| It does this | Which matters because |\n|---|---|\n| **Says when and how it was pulled** — date, window, what was excluded | Without that, nobody can reproduce it or tell whether it's stale. This one line is the difference between a document and a screenshot |\n| **Every column filled, including the hard one** | \"Optimizing to\" is the one people skip because it takes an extra click. It's also the one that matters most on an account that's already on smart bidding |\n| **Shows the reasoning, not just the answer** | \"How I told brand from non-brand\" is one sentence and it lets a reader catch you being wrong |\n| **Answers the question that was actually asked** | The brief asked for two sentences on what the account is doing and how you'd know if it stopped. They're there, at the end, in plain language |\n| **Separates observations from conclusions** | The three noticed-but-didn't-chase items are flagged as questions, not findings. That's honest and it's also how you build a list of things worth doing next |\n\n---\n\n## The weak version of the same thing\n\nFor contrast — this is what it looks like when someone does the work but not the thinking:\n\n> *\"The account has 5 active campaigns. There are search campaigns, a PMax campaign and a\n> DSA campaign. Most use tROAS bidding. Brand and non-brand are both present. Overall the\n> account is performing reasonably well.\"*\n\nEverything in it is true. It is also almost useless:\n\n- **No date and no window** — is this today or a month ago?\n- **Nothing is per-campaign**, so nobody can act on any of it\n- **\"Optimizing to\" is missing entirely** — the column that would have caught the two-action\n  problem\n- **\"Performing reasonably well\"** is a judgment with no number behind it, and nobody asked\n  for a verdict\n- **Nothing noticed, nothing questioned** — which reads as \"I looked at the surface\"\n\nThe gap between those two write-ups isn't effort or talent. It's four extra clicks and the\nwillingness to write down what you weren't sure about.\n\n---\n\n## The three things every deliverable needs\n\nWhatever the project, whatever the shape:\n\n1. **Where it came from.** Source, date, window, and anything you excluded. One line at the top.\n2. **The thing that was asked for**, in the shape it was asked for. If the brief says five\n   lines, five lines is the correct answer and eleven is a worse one.\n3. **What you weren't sure about.** The questions are not a sign you did it badly — they are\n   usually the most valuable part, and they're the difference between a deliverable and a\n   homework assignment.\n\n> **On length:** short is nearly always right. A one-page map that someone reads beats a\n> four-page one that they skim. If you're padding, stop.\n\n---\n\n## Where to put them\n\n`04-my-work/projects/`, one file per project, named after the project — `t1-01-account-tour.md`\nor similar. Uploading through the GitHub web interface is completely fine."
  },
  {
    id: "02-learning/the-l10-huddle",
    title: "The L10 huddle",
    folder: "Learning",
    kind: "Learning",
    projectId: "",
    source: "02-learning/the-l10-huddle.md",
    summary: "The team's weekly meeting, in depth — because it's the one meeting where your work becomes",
    order: 170,
    words: 1711,
    body: "# The L10 huddle\n\nThe team's weekly meeting, in depth — because it's the one meeting where your work becomes\nvisible to everyone, and because it runs on rules that aren't obvious from the outside.\n\n> **Tuesday, 10:00.** Alex facilitates. Courtney, Scott, CJ and you. It runs inside the\n> **L10 Huddle app**, which lives in the MTD Spend workbook — Alex will show you where.\n\n---\n\n## What \"L10\" means\n\nIt's short for **Level 10**, from a management system called EOS. The idea is blunt: most\nrecurring team meetings are bad, so you fix them by running the *same agenda in the same\norder every single week* and then asking everyone to rate the meeting 1–10 on the way out.\nThe name is the target. A meeting that consistently earns an 8 or better is doing its job.\n\n**Why this matters to you specifically:** in a company this size it is genuinely possible to\nwork hard for a month and have nobody know what you did. The L10 is the structural fix for\nthat. Your to-dos, your numbers and eventually your rocks are all read out loud, weekly, in\nfront of the whole team.\n\nThat sounds intimidating for about three weeks and then becomes the thing you'd miss most.\n\n---\n\n## The seven segments\n\nThe app runs a countdown on each one. The clock is the point — it's what stops the meeting\nbecoming an hour of the loudest topic.\n\n| # | Segment | Min | What actually happens |\n|---|---------|----:|----------------------|\n| 1 | **Segue** | 5 | One personal good-news item and one work one, each. Kudos. **No work talk yet** — that's deliberate, not filler |\n| 2 | **Scorecard** | 5 | The weekly numbers get read. Each is **on track or off track**. Nothing else |\n| 3 | **Rock review** | 5 | Each rock owner says on track or off track. Same rule |\n| 4 | **Headlines** | 5 | One-liners — customer news, people news, FYIs. Old news gets killed |\n| 5 | **To-dos** | 5 | Last week's list, read out: done or not done |\n| 6 | **IDS** | 60 | The actual meeting. Identify, Discuss, Solve |\n| 7 | **Conclude** | 5 | New to-dos recapped, recap posted to team chat, everyone rates the meeting |\n\n---\n\n## Segment 2 · The scorecard, and the rule people get wrong\n\nA handful of weekly numbers, each with an owner. Spend pacing per account group, efficiency\nagainst its guardrail, experiments running, negatives added and the spend that saved.\n\n**The rule: you do not discuss the scorecard during the scorecard.**\n\nIf a number is off track, you say \"off track\" and it **drops down** to the issues list for\nIDS. You don't explain it. You don't defend it. You don't solve it. That's the whole\nconversation, and it's the only reason five minutes stays five minutes.\n\nNew people find this deeply unnatural — the instinct when your number is red is to\nimmediately say why. Resist it. You'll get a proper hour on it in IDS if it's important\nenough to win a vote, and if it isn't important enough to win a vote, it didn't need the\nexplanation.\n\n> ⚠️ **Every number carries its caveat, read out with it.** Especially anything\n> conversion-flavored while tracking fixes are pending. A number without its caveat is worse\n> than no number, because people act on it. See\n> [`how-brady-measures.md`](./how-brady-measures.md).\n\n## Segment 3 · Rocks\n\nA **rock** is a quarterly commitment — something meaningful you'll finish in about 90 days.\nOne to three per person, due-dated, each tagged to one of the team's four strategic shifts.\nThey're set in a working session at the fiscal-quarter turn, not invented in the huddle.\n\nRocks carry dated milestones plotted across their life, so \"on track\" is checkable rather\nthan a feeling. Ticking the last milestone closes the rock.\n\n**You will not have a rock for a while**, and that's correct — a rock is a 90-day promise and\nyou're still learning what 90 days of your own output looks like. Expect your first one\nsomewhere after the ramp. Until then you'll hear other people's and start to learn what a\nwell-sized one looks like.\n\n## Segment 4 · Headlines\n\nOne-liners only. Something a customer said, someone's news, an FYI. If it needs a decision,\nit isn't a headline — it's an issue.\n\nAnything worth passing up to Stuart's wider digital team gets flagged to **cascade**, and\nAlex carries it there. Old headlines get killed off the list deliberately, so the list stays\na signal.\n\n## Segment 5 · To-dos\n\nLast week's list, read out: **done or not done.**\n\n| The rule | What it means |\n|---|---|\n| **One owner, seven days** | \"We'll all look at it\" means nobody will. Every to-do has exactly one name |\n| **Team target is ≥90% done** | It is a **team** number, always. There is no per-person completion percentage anywhere in the system, on purpose |\n| **\"Working on it\" is not done** | Neither is \"blocked.\" Both are legitimate — just say which one, and who you're waiting on |\n| **Three carry-overs and it gets re-filed** | The app flags it and offers two doors: make it a rock, or take it to IDS. Work that survives three weeks was never a seven-day commitment — that's a scoping problem, not a character problem |\n\n**The to-do list is a daily surface, not a Tuesday ritual.** The team spends most of its time\nin it. It has search, **Mine / Overdue / Next 7 days** filters, three states (open, working,\nblocked), snooze buttons, sub-steps for breaking a big one down, and an append-only notes\ntrail so the story of a task lives on the task instead of scattered through chat.\n\n**Marking something blocked posts to the team space**, so a hand-off doesn't sit until\nTuesday. Use it. It is not an admission of anything.\n\n## Segment 6 · IDS — the hour that matters\n\nSixty of the ninety minutes. **Identify, Discuss, Solve.**\n\n**How it works:** issues have accumulated all week — dropped down from the scorecard, raised\nby anyone, or added from a desk on a Thursday. Then:\n\n1. **Voting.** The app picks someone at random to start, then goes around the room in turn.\n   **Three votes each**, stacked on one issue or spread across three. The list re-orders\n   itself by votes.\n2. **Take the top one.** **Identify** the actual root cause — usually not the thing that was\n   written down. **Discuss** it once, properly. **Solve** it.\n3. **The solution becomes a to-do** with one owner and a date.\n4. Repeat down the list until the hour goes.\n\n**\"Solve forever, not for this week.\"** If the fix wouldn't stop it happening again, it isn't\na solve — it's a patch, and the issue will be back next month wearing a different hat.\n\nThree things that keep IDS honest:\n\n- **An issue is not a status update.** If it doesn't need a decision or a solution, it's a\n  headline.\n- **Two-person topics get parked to a 1:1.** A thing that only concerns you and Alex should\n  not spend four people's hour. The app parks it to that 1:1 for you.\n- **If the answer is \"we should test that,\"** it goes to the Experiment Hub's ideas backlog,\n  which is where tests actually live. See\n  [`running-a-real-test.md`](./running-a-real-test.md).\n\n**You get three votes from your first meeting.** Not after you've earned them. If you don't\nyet know what matters most, vote for the thing you understood least — that's genuinely useful\ninformation for the room.\n\n## Segment 7 · Conclude\n\nNew to-dos read back, the recap goes to the team chat, anything cascading is set, and\neveryone rates the meeting 1–10.\n\n**Rate it honestly.** Below an 8, you say where it lost you. A new person saying \"6, I didn't\nfollow the second half\" is not a complaint — it's the single most useful data point the\nfacilitator gets, because everyone else has stopped noticing.\n\n---\n\n## The house rules, in one place\n\n1. The scorecard never gets discussed in the scorecard segment. \"Drop it down\" is the whole conversation.\n2. Every number carries its caveat.\n3. One owner per to-do, seven days.\n4. Issues are not status updates.\n5. Two-person topics get parked to a 1:1.\n6. Solved means written down — resolution logged, actions owned and dated.\n7. Test-shaped issues go to the Experiment Hub.\n8. **Off track is information, not an accusation.**\n\nThat last one is the culture of the meeting and it's worth reading twice. **Saying \"off\ntrack\" early is the move that earns trust here.** Surprises at the deadline are what cost it.\nNobody in this room is impressed by a number that was green for six weeks and then wasn't.\n\n---\n\n## What's expected of you, by when\n\n| Phase | What you do in the huddle |\n|-------|--------------------------|\n| **Weeks 1–4** | Sit in. Listen. Bring a segue item — that one you can do from day one, and it's how the room learns who you are. Ask afterward about anything you didn't follow |\n| **Weeks 4–8** | You'll start carrying to-dos of your own. Read your own status honestly. Start voting in IDS |\n| **Weeks 8–13** | You own a line on the scorecard and read your own number. You raise issues rather than only answering them |\n| **After the ramp** | A rock of your own. You bring solutions to IDS rather than only problems |\n\n---\n\n## The two mistakes new people make\n\n**1. Explaining a red number during the scorecard.** Covered above. Everyone does it once.\n\n**2. Marking a to-do done when it's mostly done.** The ≥90% target creates a small, real pull\ntoward optimism. Don't. A team number that's honest is useful and a team number that's\nflattering is worse than nothing — and the whole reason the target is a *team* number, with\nno per-person breakdown anywhere, is so that nobody has an incentive to fudge their own row.\n\n---\n\n## Related\n\n- [`../01-start-here/your-week.md`](../01-start-here/your-week.md) — where the huddle sits\n  among the other recurring meetings\n- [`how-brady-measures.md`](./how-brady-measures.md) — the caveats behind the scorecard numbers"
  },
  {
    id: "02-learning/who-else-is-searching",
    title: "Who else is searching",
    folder: "Learning",
    kind: "Learning",
    projectId: "",
    source: "02-learning/who-else-is-searching.md",
    summary: "The people who trigger our ads without ever intending to buy anything — and the ones who",
    order: 180,
    words: 1838,
    body: "# Who else is searching\n\nThe people who trigger our ads without ever intending to buy anything — and the ones who\nlook like them but aren't.\n\n> **Read this before your first real search-terms round** ([T2-1](../03-projects/t2-01-sqr-predict-then-compare.md)).\n> [T1-3](../03-projects/t1-03-keyword-vs-search-term.md) teaches you the mechanics of a\n> search terms report. This is about what you're looking *at* once you can read one.\n\n---\n\n## The point\n\nA search terms report is a list of what real people typed. Most of the bad rows aren't\nrandom — they fall into a handful of recognizable groups, and once you can name the group\nyou can spot the next one in about two seconds instead of thinking it through from scratch.\n\n**The categories are the skill.** Anyone can look at \"brady bobblehead\" and know it's wrong.\nThe useful version is recognizing it as *the same thing* as \"seton hall admissions\" — a name\ncollision — because then you know to go looking for the rest of them.\n\n---\n\n## The archetypes\n\n| Who | What you'll see | Waste? |\n|-----|-----------------|--------|\n| **Name collisions** | Searches for a completely different organization that happens to share our name | **Yes, always.** See the section below — this is the big one here |\n| **Job seekers** | `jobs`, `careers`, `salary`, `hiring`, `resume`, `internship`, `glassdoor` | **Yes.** Brady is a large employer and people search for it as one |\n| **Students and researchers** | `what is`, `meaning`, `definition`, `requirements`, `standard`, `training`, `certification` — often attached to a compliance standard rather than a product | **Usually.** But see \"the ones that look like waste\" below |\n| **Template and freebie hunters** | `free`, `printable`, `template`, `download`, `pdf`, `clip art` | **Usually yes.** Someone printing their own sign at home is not going to order from us |\n| **Consumer and hobbyist** | `home`, `diy`, `craft`, `cheap`, `personal`, `for my` | **Depends on the account.** See the warning below — this is the one that catches people out |\n| **Competitor and comparison** | Named competitors, `vs`, `alternative to`, `review` | **Not automatically.** Some of this is deliberate. Ask before you touch it |\n| **Support and existing customers** | `manual`, `driver`, `login`, `return`, `warranty`, `how to replace` | **Usually.** They're already customers — we're paying to send them somewhere they could have found free |\n\n---\n\n## ⚠️ Name collisions: the one that will surprise you\n\n**\"Brady\" is not a reliable signal that someone means Brady.**\n\nThere is a real shared negative list in the account called **`Famous Brady Terms`**. Open it\nand you'll find `bucs`, `buccaneers`, `bobblehead`, `tampa` and `coach`.\n\nIt's about **Tom Brady**. An entire negative list exists because a football player has the\nsame name as the company.\n\nThat's funny, and it's also the most useful single fact on this page: **a brand name is not\na brand signal.** Any time you're about to treat a name as proof of intent, ask what else\nhas that name.\n\n**Now go and do it for Seton.** Seton is also a university, a hospital system and the name\nof a great many schools. Before your first negatives round, find out:\n\n1. Are those searches reaching our Seton campaigns at all?\n2. If they are, are they already blocked, or has nobody looked?\n\nDon't take my word for either answer — go and look. That's the actual exercise, and it's the\nkind of thing a new person finds because they haven't stopped seeing it yet.\n\n---\n\n## ⚠️ The ones that look like waste and aren't\n\nThis is where a new analyst does real damage. Over-blocking is quiet — traffic just stops\narriving and nothing tells you why.\n\n### Half the \"negative\" lists aren't blocking waste\n\nA large share of Brady's shared negative lists exist to **route traffic between campaigns**\nrather than to stop it — keeping brand searches out of non-brand campaigns, or steering\nproducts toward the right Shopping campaign. Open one and you'll see terms for things Brady\nvery much sells.\n\nIf you add to one of those thinking it's a hygiene list, you don't remove waste. You send\ntraffic somewhere you didn't intend, and nothing announces it. **Read the terms in a list\nbefore you add to it** — if they're things we sell, it's routing, not blocking. This is also\ncovered in [`before-it-ships.md`](./before-it-ships.md).\n\n### Research intent isn't waste in a long sales cycle\n\nSomeone searching a compliance standard rather than a product might be a student. They might\nalso be the facility manager who will spec our signs in five months. B2B buying cycles are\nlong enough that the first search and the purchase can look like different people.\n\n**The tell is usually the rest of the phrase**, not the research word itself. Someone asking\nwhat a standard *means* is reading. Someone asking what a standard *requires for their site*\nis scoping a job.\n\n### \"Consumer\" depends on the account\n\nThe consumer/hobbyist archetype is the one that gets misapplied here, because our accounts\ndon't all sell the same way. Wristbands in particular has a much broader buyer than the\nindustrial accounts do.\n\n**Before you treat consumer-sounding traffic as junk on any account, check what that account\nactually sells.** The archetype is a hypothesis, not a verdict.\n\n---\n\n## ⚠️ The B2B gray zone — not junk, just not your buyer\n\nEverything above covers searches that are clearly *wrong* — name collisions, job seekers,\ntemplate hunters. This section is about the harder call: **searches that are commercially\nlegitimate but the person behind them is almost certainly not a B2B buyer.**\n\nThis is the biggest waste category on accounts like PDC and BradyCorp non-brand, and it's\nthe hardest one to see because the search terms look fine.\n\n### The problem\n\nSomeone types `safety signs`. That's a real product Brady sells. But who typed it?\n\n| Who they might be | What they'd buy | Brady's fit |\n|---|---|---|\n| A facility manager outfitting a warehouse | 500 custom OSHA signs, $3,000+ order | **Exactly our buyer** |\n| A small business owner who needs one \"Employees Only\" sign | 1 sign, $8, from Amazon | **Not our buyer** — our minimum order, lead time and price point don't work for them |\n| A teacher making a classroom display | Nothing | **Not our buyer** |\n| A homeowner who wants a \"No Trespassing\" sign for their yard | 1 sign, hardware store | **Not our buyer** |\n\nAll four typed the same thing. The first one is worth $3,000. The other three cost you a\nclick each and will never convert — not because your ad was bad, but because **you don't\nsell the way they buy.**\n\n### Why this is different from the junk archetypes\n\nThe junk archetypes (job seekers, template hunters) are easy: the *search term itself* tells\nyou the intent is wrong. \"Safety sign template\" — kill it, no judgment call needed.\n\nThe B2B gray zone is harder because **the search term is exactly right.** `Safety signs` is\nwhat you sell. The problem isn't the term, it's the *person* — and you can't see the person\nin a search terms report.\n\n### Signals that a search is probably consumer, not B2B\n\nYou can't know for sure, but patterns help:\n\n| Signal | What it suggests | Example |\n|---|---|---|\n| **\"for home,\" \"for my,\" \"personal,\" \"small\"** | Individual buyer, not procurement | `small no smoking sign for home` |\n| **A modifier that implies one unit** | Not a commercial order | `custom name badge` (vs. `custom name badges bulk`) |\n| **\"near me\" on a product we ship** | Looking for a retail store, not a manufacturer | `safety signs near me` |\n| **\"Amazon,\" \"Walmart,\" \"Home Depot\"** | Shopping for a retail channel we don't own (or compete on differently) | `safety signs home depot` |\n| **\"cheap,\" \"affordable,\" \"under $10\"** | Price point below our minimum viable order | `cheap caution signs` |\n| **A very generic, high-volume term** | Broad audience, low B2B concentration | `signs` (800K monthly searches, almost none B2B) |\n\n### What to do about it\n\nThis is **not** a \"kill them all\" situation. You can't negative-match your way out of the\ngray zone without also blocking the facility manager who types the same words. Instead:\n\n1. **Negative the clear consumer modifiers** — `for home`, `near me`, `cheap`, `diy`,\n   specific retail competitors. These are safe to block.\n2. **Use the search terms report to size the problem.** What share of non-brand spend goes\n   to broad, generic terms where the B2B/consumer mix is likely unfavorable? That's a\n   structural question about whether those terms are worth running, not a per-term\n   Keep/Kill.\n3. **Watch match types.** Broad match on a term like `safety signs` will pull in consumer\n   queries that phrase or exact won't. If the gray zone is big on a particular term,\n   tightening match type is a lever — at the cost of volume. This is a real tradeoff, not a\n   clear answer.\n4. **Landing pages and pricing do the final filtering.** Someone who lands on a page that\n   says \"Minimum order: 50 units\" self-selects out if they wanted one sign. That's not a\n   paid-search fix, but it's worth knowing it's there.\n5. **Audience signals can help on PMax.** If the campaign has audience signals pointing at\n   B2B in-market segments, Google is more likely to serve to the facility manager and less\n   likely to serve to the homeowner. This doesn't guarantee it — signals are hints, not\n   targeting.\n\n### The mindset shift\n\nOn a consumer account, every click from a real shopper is potentially a conversion. On a\nB2B account like PDC or BradyCorp non-brand, **a meaningful share of commercially\nlegitimate-looking clicks will never convert because the business model doesn't serve that\nbuyer.** That's not waste you can eliminate — it's a structural cost of advertising on\nshared search terms. The job is to minimize it without killing the real demand that uses the\nsame words.\n\n---\n\n## Using this on a real report\n\n1. **Sort the junk into groups before you judge any of it.** Naming the group first is\n   faster and it stops you making the same call twelve different ways.\n2. **Ask what the group has in common** — that's what becomes a negative, not the individual\n   term. One good negative kills a hundred future bad searches. A hundred exact-match\n   negatives kill a hundred searches that already happened.\n3. **Then check the group against the two warnings above** before anything ships.\n\n## ⚠️ One last thing about those identifier words\n\nThe words in the table above are **hints for finding groups, not negatives to paste in.**\n\n`free` as a broad negative also blocks `free shipping safety signs`. `coach` blocks\n`coaching whistle` if we ever sold one. `home` blocks `nursing home`, which on a healthcare\naccount is exactly the customer you want.\n\nEvery one of those is a real way to delete good traffic silently. Match type is the whole\ngame here — see [T2-3](../03-projects/t2-03-negative-keyword-build-v1.md) and the negatives\nsection of [`before-it-ships.md`](./before-it-ships.md)."
  },
